# Trivy misconfiguration checks suppressed repo-wide.
#
# Trivy auto-detects this file (it is the default --ignorefile); the
# org-shared security-scan workflow passes no ignore path, so anything
# recorded here must live at the repo root under this exact name. The
# `.trivyignore.yaml` variant is NOT auto-detected and would silently do
# nothing.
#
# Only checks that cannot produce signal against this repository belong
# here. A check that is merely inconvenient to satisfy stays enabled and is
# tracked as work instead — suppressing it would hide the finding rather
# than answer it.

# limit range usage
#
# The check requires a LimitRange object in the SAME scanned file as the
# namespaced resource it is judging. It has no view of the namespace, so it
# can never observe the LimitRange that actually applies. Two proofs that
# this is a file-scope artefact rather than a coverage gap: it fires on the
# limit-range.yaml manifests that themselves define a LimitRange, and it
# fires on HTTPRoutes, ExternalSecrets, ServiceMonitors and PrometheusRules,
# none of which consume resource limits at all.
#
# The real control is the per-namespace LimitRange applied by the tenant
# envelope. Every namespace defined by a tenant.yaml carries one; kube-system
# is excluded deliberately via `noLimitRange`, and default/kube-public/
# kube-node-lease hold no workloads.
KSV-0039

# Workloads in the default namespace
#
# Fires only on chart templates under helm-charts/ and on postInstall
# manifests, neither of which sets metadata.namespace — correct in both
# cases, because the namespace comes from the Helm release or the ArgoCD
# Application destination declared in tenant.yaml. Trivy renders templates
# with no release namespace, so the absent field reads as "default". No
# workload in this repo is deployed to the default namespace.
KSV-0110

# Restrict container images to trusted registries
#
# The check ships with no registry allowlist and takes one only through Rego
# data supplied at invocation. The org-shared workflow passes none, so the
# trusted set is empty and every image fails regardless of origin —
# including images already pinned by digest and images from ghcr.io. A
# result that is identical for every input carries no signal.
#
# Image provenance in this repo is enforced instead by
# tools/check-image-pins.py, which requires a digest anchor on every
# reference under tenants/ and helm-charts/ and admits exceptions only with
# a written reason. Pinning by digest is a strictly stronger guarantee than
# allowlisting the registry a mutable tag is served from.
#
# Remove this entry if the shared workflow is ever given a populated
# trusted-registry set, at which point the check starts discriminating.
KSV-0125
